module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.LocallyInjective
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.EpiLocallySurjective
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.KernelIdealSheaf
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SubmoduleFactorThru
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ULiftUnit
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SubmoduleKer
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheaf

/-!
# The quotient of a sheaf of rings by a sheaf of ideals, at the level of sheaves

Let `O` be a sheaf of commutative rings on a site and let `I` be a sheaf of ideals of `O`. The
quotient `O/I` of `Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheaf` is the sheafification of the objectwise
quotient, and the surjectivity and kernel statements available for it are objectwise: the sections
of `O/I` over an object of the site are *not* the quotient of the sections of `O` there, because a
section of the quotient need only locally come from a section of `O`.

This file proves the two corresponding statements for the sheaves themselves. The quotient map

`O ⟶ O/I`

is locally surjective, and the kernel ideal of the induced morphism of sheaves of rings is `I` on
the nose. Read in sheaves of `O`-modules, the quotient map is therefore an epimorphism whose
kernel ideal is `I`. Since the inclusion of `I` is then a kernel of an epimorphism, the quotient
map is a cokernel of that inclusion, which identifies `O/I` with the quotient of `O` by `I` formed
in the abelian category of sheaves of `O`-modules. Those are the two quotients a sheaf of ideals
carries, and nothing compared them before.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Sites.cokernelIsoQuotientRing`: the comparison between the two quotients of `O` by a
  sheaf of ideals — the one formed in sheaves of `O`-modules, as the cokernel of the inclusion of
  `I`, and the sheafification of the objectwise quotient *ring*.
* `Stacks.Sites.idealOfHom` and `Stacks.Sites.cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom`: the sheaf of ideals
  generated by a morphism into the structure sheaf, and the identification of the cokernel of
  that morphism with the quotient ring sheaf by it.

## Main statements

* `Stacks.Sites.isLocallySurjective_toQuotientRingSheaf`: the quotient map `O ⟶ O/I` is locally
  surjective.
* `Stacks.Sites.isLocallySurjective_quotientRingSheafMapOfLE` and
  `Stacks.Sites.isLocallySurjective_adicQuotientMap`: so is every comparison `O/I ⟶ O/I'` of an
  inclusion of sheaves of ideals, and in particular every map `O/Iⁿ ⟶ O/Iᵐ` of the adic tower.
* `Stacks.Sites.isLocallySurjective_sheafCompose_forget₂_ringCat`: local surjectivity is not lost
  by forgetting commutativity, which is the spelling in which a sheaf of modules names its
  coefficient sheaf.
* `Stacks.Sites.kerIdeal_quotientRingHom`: the kernel ideal of `O ⟶ O/I` is `I`.
* `Stacks.Sites.epi_unitToQuotientRing`: the quotient map, read as a morphism of sheaves of
  `O`-modules, is an epimorphism.
* `Stacks.Sites.ker_unitToQuotientRing`: `I` is the kernel of that morphism, as a submodule of
  the free rank-one sheaf of modules.

## Implementation notes

**Local surjectivity is a composite.** The quotient map is by construction the objectwise
quotient map followed by sheafification. The first is surjective on every object, hence locally
surjective; the second is locally surjective because sheafification is. Local surjectivity is
closed under composition, so nothing else is needed.

**The comparisons of the tower cost nothing more.** Composing `O/I ⟶ O/I'` with `O ⟶ O/I` gives
`O ⟶ O/I'`, which is locally surjective by the above, and a composite can only be locally
surjective if its *second* factor is
(`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_of_isLocallySurjective_fac`, which needs nothing of
the first factor). This is what makes the hypothesis of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ChangeOfRings.essImage_restrictScalars_iff` available at every stage of
the adic tower, and not only at `O ⟶ O/Iⁿ`.

**Forgetting commutativity is invisible to local surjectivity.** A sheaf of modules names its
coefficient sheaf as a sheaf of not necessarily commutative rings, so the hypothesis is wanted
for the image of the quotient map under `sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)`. That
image has the same components as the map itself, so it has the same image sieves, and the
statement is the identity on witnesses rather than a transport along
`Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_iff_whisker_forget`.

**The kernel is where the sheaf condition on `I` is used.** A section `a` of `O` over `X` dies in
`O/I` exactly when its class in the objectwise quotient dies under sheafification, and
sheafification is locally *injective*, so that happens exactly when `a` lies in `I` locally. The
sheaf condition on `I` — membership in a sheaf of ideals is local, which is the defining property
of `SheafOfModules.Submodule` — turns that into membership of `a` in `I` over `X`. This is the one
step that the objectwise statements cannot see, and it is why the objectwise kernel is the ideal
of sections while the sheaf-level kernel is the sheaf of ideals.

**Transporting local surjectivity between the ring and the module reading.** Local surjectivity of
a morphism of presheaves with values in a concrete category depends only on the underlying
functions: the sieve `Presheaf.imageSieve` is defined from them, and
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.imageSieve_whisker_forget` says so. The morphism of sheaves of
`O`-modules induced by `O ⟶ O/I` has the same components as the morphism of sheaves of rings, so
its image sieves are literally the same sieves and the covering property transfers with no
comparison of sheafification in `CommRingCat` with sheafification in `AddCommGrpCat`.

**The universes are pinned to the diagonal.** The local injectivity and local surjectivity of
sheafification for a concrete category are available when the category's universe is that of the
site's objects and morphisms jointly. Taking the site to be a `Category.{u}` on a `Type u` and the
rings to be `CommRingCat.{u}` satisfies this, and it is the situation of the site of opens of a
topological space, which is where this is used.

**Apply the comparison without restating its type.** The target of
`cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom` names the quotient ring sheaf, whose construction unfolds to a
sheafification. Over a general site, checking a hand-written copy of that type against the
inferred one is cheap; over a concrete site such as the opens of a topological space it exhausts
the unifier, while elaborating the term and letting its type be inferred is immediate. So a
downstream use should name the isomorphism and let its type be inferred rather than restate it.

The rule has a boundary, and it is worth knowing: it is about types valued in a **sheafification**,
not about large or awkward types in general. Elsewhere in this development, statements whose types
are written out in full — the stalk identifications of the Ext sheaves, whose types name a derived
Ext sheaf and an exterior power — elaborate without trouble. So explicit types should not be
avoided everywhere at the cost of readability; they should be avoided where the type names a
sheafified object.

**Hand-writing an inferable type can break instance search, which is a different failure.** The
note above is about the unifier: a restated target type valued in a sheafification exhausts it.
A distinct failure, with the same remedy, is that writing out the binder types of a statement can
stop a `Module` or `HSMul` instance from resolving at all. Concretely, in the Ext descent the
annihilation statement over an object `Y` of the opposite category, with the scalar typed as
`(toRingCat J O).obj.obj Y`, made the action on a section of an internal Hom sheaf unresolvable:
instance search failed outright, with no timeout. The same statement with the object written as
`op U` for `U` in the category, and the scalar typed as `O.obj.obj (op U)`, resolves immediately.
The two forms are definitionally equal, so the difference is purely in what instance search can
see. The discipline is the same in both cases — let the elaborator infer — but the symptoms are
not: a timeout in the unifier in one case, an unresolved instance in the other.

**Two spellings of the sheaf of rings, again.** `Stacks.Sites.IdealSubsheaf` is phrased with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf`, so the inclusion of a sheaf of
ideals lands in the free rank-one module over that spelling, and this file stays in it
throughout. The Koszul development works over the reducible `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat`
instead. The two are definitionally equal and a morphism written in either spelling is accepted
by the results below; what is not accepted is a restated target type, for the reason just given.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01AG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01AG)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

universe u

namespace Stacks.Sites


variable {C : Type u} [Category.{u} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
  [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})]
  {O : Sheaf J CommRingCat.{u}} [HasWeakSheafify J CommRingCat.{u}]

/-- **The quotient map `O ⟶ O/I` is locally surjective.** It is the objectwise quotient map,
which is surjective over every object of the site, followed by sheafification, which is locally
surjective. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_toQuotientRingSheaf (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J (toQuotientRingSheaf I).hom := by
  have h1 : Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J (toQuotientPresheaf I) :=
    Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_of_surjective J (toQuotientPresheaf I)
      (toQuotientPresheaf_app_surjective I)
  show Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J
    (toQuotientPresheaf I ≫ toSheafify J (quotientPresheaf I))
  infer_instance

/-- **The comparison `O/I ⟶ O/I'` of an inclusion of sheaves of ideals is locally surjective.**
Composing it with `O ⟶ O/I` gives `O ⟶ O/I'`, which is locally surjective, and a composite can
only be locally surjective if its second factor is. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_quotientRingSheafMapOfLE {I I' : IdealSubsheaf O} (h : I ≤ I') :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J (quotientRingSheafMapOfLE h).hom := by
  have fac : (toQuotientRingSheaf I).hom ≫ (quotientRingSheafMapOfLE h).hom =
      (toQuotientRingSheaf I').hom := by
    rw [← toQuotientRingSheaf_comp_quotientRingSheafMapOfLE h]
    rfl
  have := isLocallySurjective_toQuotientRingSheaf I'
  exact Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_of_isLocallySurjective_fac J fac

/-- The quotient map `O ⟶ O/Iⁿ` of the adic tower is locally surjective. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_toAdicQuotient (I : IdealSubsheaf O) (n : ℕ) :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J (toAdicQuotient I n).hom :=
  isLocallySurjective_toQuotientRingSheaf _

/-- **The map `O/Iⁿ ⟶ O/Iᵐ` of the adic tower is locally surjective**, for `m ≤ n`. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_adicQuotientMap (I : IdealSubsheaf O) {m n : ℕ} (h : m ≤ n) :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J (adicQuotientMap I h).hom :=
  isLocallySurjective_quotientRingSheafMapOfLE _

omit [HasWeakSheafify J CommRingCat.{u}] in
/-- **Local surjectivity survives forgetting commutativity.** A locally surjective map of sheaves
of commutative rings is locally surjective as a map of sheaves of rings, which is the spelling in
which a sheaf of modules names its coefficient sheaf: the two morphisms have the same components,
hence the same image sieves. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_sheafCompose_forget₂_ringCat {R R' : Sheaf J CommRingCat.{u}}
    (α : R ⟶ R') (hα : Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J α.hom) :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J
      ((sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).map α).hom where
  imageSieve_mem {_X} y := hα.imageSieve_mem y

/-- The quotient map `O ⟶ O/I`, read as a morphism of the underlying sheaves of rings. -/
def quotientRingHom (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O ⟶
      Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (quotientRingSheaf I) :=
  (sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).map (toQuotientRingSheaf I)

/-- **The kernel ideal of the quotient map `O ⟶ O/I` is `I`.** A section of `O` dies in `O/I`
exactly when it lies in `I` locally, because sheafification is locally injective; and membership
in a sheaf of ideals is local, so such a section lies in `I`. -/
theorem kerIdeal_quotientRingHom (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    SheafOfModules.kerIdeal (quotientRingHom I) = I :=
  SheafOfModules.Submodule.ext (PresheafOfModules.Submodule.ext fun X =>
    SetLike.ext fun a => by
      refine Iff.trans (SheafOfModules.mem_kerIdeal (quotientRingHom I) X a) ?_
      refine Iff.trans RingHom.mem_ker ?_
      refine ⟨fun ha => ?_, fun ha => ?_⟩
      · refine I.isSheaf a (J.superset_covering ?_ (Presheaf.equalizerSieve_mem J
          (toSheafify J (quotientPresheaf I)) ((toQuotientPresheaf I).app X a) 0 (by
            simp only [map_zero]; exact ha)))
        intro V f hf
        show (O.obj.map f.op) a ∈ I.obj (op V)
        rw [← mem_sectionIdeal, ← Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem]
        have hf' : (quotientPresheaf I).map f.op ((toQuotientPresheaf I).app X a) = 0 := by
          simpa using hf
        exact (ConcreteCategory.congr_hom ((toQuotientPresheaf I).naturality f.op) a).symm.trans hf'
      · show (toSheafify J (quotientPresheaf I)).app X ((toQuotientPresheaf I).app X a) = 0
        rw [show (toQuotientPresheaf I).app X a = 0 from Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.2 ha,
          map_zero])

/-- The quotient map `O ⟶ O/I`, read as a morphism of sheaves of `O`-modules from the free
rank-one module to `O/I` with scalars restricted along the quotient map. -/
def unitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O) ⟶
      (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (quotientRingHom I)).obj
        (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (quotientRingSheaf I))) :=
  SheafOfModules.unitToRestrictScalarsObjUnit (quotientRingHom I)

/-- **The quotient map is locally surjective as a morphism of sheaves of modules.** Its
components are those of the morphism of sheaves of rings, so its image sieves are the same
sieves. -/
theorem isLocallySurjective_unitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    Presheaf.IsLocallySurjective J
      ((PresheafOfModules.toPresheaf (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O).obj).map
        (unitToQuotientRing I).val) where
  imageSieve_mem {_X} y :=
    have := isLocallySurjective_toQuotientRingSheaf I
    Presheaf.imageSieve_mem J (toQuotientRingSheaf I).hom y

/-- **The quotient map `O ⟶ O/I` is an epimorphism of sheaves of `O`-modules.** -/
theorem epi_unitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) : Epi (unitToQuotientRing I) :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.EpiMono.epi_of_isLocallySurjective _
    (isLocallySurjective_unitToQuotientRing I)

/-- **`I` is the kernel of the quotient map, as a submodule.** -/
theorem ker_unitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    SheafOfModules.Submodule.ker (unitToQuotientRing I) = I :=
  SheafOfModules.Submodule.ext (PresheafOfModules.Submodule.ext fun X =>
    SetLike.ext fun a => by
      refine Iff.trans (SheafOfModules.Submodule.mem_ker (unitToQuotientRing I) X a) ?_
      refine Iff.trans ?_ (SetLike.ext_iff.1
        (congrArg (fun N : IdealSubsheaf O => N.obj X) (kerIdeal_quotientRingHom I)) a)
      exact (Iff.trans (SheafOfModules.mem_kerIdeal (quotientRingHom I) X a)
        RingHom.mem_ker).symm)

/-- A section of `I` is annihilated by the quotient map. -/
theorem app_mem_ker_of_mem (I : IdealSubsheaf O) (X : Cᵒᵖ)
    {a : (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)).val.obj X}
    (ha : a ∈ I.obj X) :
    (unitToQuotientRing I).val.app X a = 0 :=
  (SheafOfModules.Submodule.mem_ker (unitToQuotientRing I) X a).1
    (by rwa [ker_unitToQuotientRing])

section Cokernel

variable [HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]

omit [HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}] in
/-- The inclusion of `I` into the free rank-one sheaf of modules is annihilated by the quotient
map. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem ι_comp_unitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    I.ι ≫ unitToQuotientRing I = 0 := by
  ext X x
  exact app_mem_ker_of_mem I X x.2

/-- **The inclusion of `I` is a kernel of the quotient map.** A morphism annihilated by the
quotient map takes all its values in `I`, because `I` is the kernel submodule, so it factors
through `I`; the factorization is unique because the inclusion of a submodule is a
monomorphism. -/
noncomputable def isLimitKernelForkUnitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    IsLimit (KernelFork.ofι I.ι (ι_comp_unitToQuotientRing I)) :=
  KernelFork.IsLimit.ofι' _ _ fun {_P} k hk ↦
    ⟨I.factorThru k fun X x ↦ by
        have h : PresheafOfModules.Hom.app k.val X x ∈
            (SheafOfModules.Submodule.ker (unitToQuotientRing I)).obj X :=
          (SheafOfModules.Submodule.mem_ker _ X _).2 (by
            rw [show PresheafOfModules.Hom.app (unitToQuotientRing I).val X
                (PresheafOfModules.Hom.app k.val X x)
              = PresheafOfModules.Hom.app (k ≫ unitToQuotientRing I).val X x from rfl, hk]
            rfl)
        rwa [ker_unitToQuotientRing] at h,
      I.factorThru_ι k _⟩

/-- **The quotient map exhibits `O/I` as the cokernel of the inclusion of `I`.** The inclusion of
`I` is a kernel of the quotient map and the quotient map is an epimorphism, so in an abelian
category the quotient map is a cokernel of that inclusion. -/
noncomputable def isColimitCokernelCoforkUnitToQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    IsColimit (CokernelCofork.ofπ (unitToQuotientRing I) (ι_comp_unitToQuotientRing I)) :=
  haveI : Epi (unitToQuotientRing I) := epi_unitToQuotientRing I
  Abelian.epiIsCokernelOfKernel _ (isLimitKernelForkUnitToQuotientRing I)

/-- **The quotient of `O` by a sheaf of ideals, formed in sheaves of `O`-modules, is `O/I`.**
The cokernel of the inclusion of `I` into the free rank-one sheaf of modules is the sheaf of
rings `O/I` with its scalars restricted along the quotient map.

This is the comparison between the two quotients available for a sheaf of ideals: the one formed
in the abelian category of sheaves of `O`-modules, and the sheafification of the objectwise
quotient *ring*. -/
noncomputable def cokernelIsoQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    cokernel I.ι ≅
      (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (quotientRingHom I)).obj
        (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (quotientRingSheaf I))) :=
  (cokernelIsCokernel I.ι).coconePointUniqueUpToIso
    (isColimitCokernelCoforkUnitToQuotientRing I)

/-- The comparison carries the projection onto the cokernel to the quotient map, so it identifies
*the canonical map* and not merely the two objects. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoQuotientRing (I : IdealSubsheaf O) :
    cokernel.π I.ι ≫ (cokernelIsoQuotientRing I).hom = unitToQuotientRing I :=
  (cokernelIsCokernel I.ι).comp_coconePointUniqueUpToIso_hom
    (isColimitCokernelCoforkUnitToQuotientRing I) WalkingParallelPair.one

/-! ### The quotient by the ideal generated by a morphism -/

/-- **The sheaf of ideals generated by a morphism into the structure sheaf**: the local closure
of its objectwise image. -/
noncomputable def idealOfHom {F : SheafOfModules.{u} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)}
    (φ : F ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)) : IdealSubsheaf O :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.localClosure _ (Stacks.SheafOfModules.presheafRangeSubmodule φ.val)

/-- **The cokernel of a morphism into the structure sheaf is the quotient ring sheaf by the
sheaf of ideals that the morphism generates.** A morphism factors as an epimorphism onto the
sheaf of ideals it generates followed by the inclusion of that sheaf of ideals, and a cokernel is
unchanged by precomposition with an epimorphism; so the cokernel of the morphism is the cokernel
of the inclusion, which is the quotient ring sheaf. -/
noncomputable def cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom
    {F : SheafOfModules.{u} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)}
    (φ : F ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)) :
    cokernel φ ≅
      (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (quotientRingHom (idealOfHom φ))).obj
        (SheafOfModules.unit
          (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (quotientRingSheaf (idealOfHom φ)))) :=
  haveI : Epi (Stacks.SheafOfModules.toLocalClosureRange φ) :=
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.epi_toLocalClosureRange φ
  cokernelIsoOfEq (Stacks.SheafOfModules.toLocalClosureRange_ι φ).symm ≪≫
    cokernelEpiComp (Stacks.SheafOfModules.toLocalClosureRange φ) (idealOfHom φ).ι ≪≫
    cokernelIsoQuotientRing (idealOfHom φ)

/-- **The comparison for the ideal generated by a morphism carries the projection onto the
cokernel to the quotient map.** This is the `OfHom` counterpart of
`Stacks.Sites.cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoQuotientRing`, and like it identifies *the canonical map*
and not merely the two objects.

`cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom` is a composite of three isomorphisms, and each carries the
projection onto the cokernel to the next projection: the transport along an equality of the two
morphisms does, by `CategoryTheory.Limits.π_comp_cokernelIsoOfEq_hom`; the invariance of a
cokernel under precomposition with an epimorphism does, its forward map being a `cokernel.desc`,
so `CategoryTheory.Limits.cokernel.π_desc` applies; and the comparison with the quotient ring
sheaf does, by the lemma this one is named after.

The three are composed against the explicit composite rather than by rewriting inside the
isomorphism: `Iso.trans_hom` does not fire on the nested `≪≫` here, while the hom of a composite
of isomorphisms is the composite of their homs definitionally, so stating the chain and closing
with `exact` costs nothing. The two steps are applied in their reassociated form, since
`Category.assoc` likewise does not match against the composite that the first rewrite leaves. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom
    {F : SheafOfModules.{u} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)}
    (φ : F ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)) :
    cokernel.π φ ≫ (cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom φ).hom =
      unitToQuotientRing (idealOfHom φ) := by
  have := Stacks.SheafOfModules.epi_toLocalClosureRange φ
  exact (π_comp_cokernelIsoOfEq_hom_assoc _ _).trans
    ((cokernel.π_desc_assoc _ _ _ _).trans (cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoQuotientRing _))

end Cokernel

end Stacks.Sites

end
